1. Logical Address to Physical Address Using Base and Limit Registers
Aim

To design and implement a program that translates a logical address into a physical address using base and limit registers, and checks whether the logical address is valid.

Concept
Base register: Contains the starting physical address of a process.
Limit register: Specifies the maximum valid logical address/range.
If:
Logical Address<Limit

then the address is valid.

The physical address is calculated as:

Physical Address=Base+Logical Address

If the logical address is greater than or equal to the limit, it is invalid.

Algorithm
Read the base register value.
Read the limit register value.
Read the logical address.
Check whether logical_address < limit.
If valid, calculate physical_address = base + logical_address.
Otherwise, display an invalid-address message.
Stop.
C Program
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int base, limit, logicalAddress, physicalAddress;

    printf("Enter Base Register value: ");
    scanf("%d", &base);

    printf("Enter Limit Register value: ");
    scanf("%d", &limit);

    printf("Enter Logical Address: ");
    scanf("%d", &logicalAddress);

    if (logicalAddress >= 0 && logicalAddress < limit) {
        physicalAddress = base + logicalAddress;

        printf("\nAddress Translation Successful\n");
        printf("Logical Address  : %d\n", logicalAddress);
        printf("Base Register    : %d\n", base);
        printf("Limit Register   : %d\n", limit);
        printf("Physical Address : %d\n", physicalAddress);
    } else {
        printf("\nInvalid Logical Address!\n");
        printf("The address is outside the allowed range.\n");
    }

    return 0;
}
