4. Translation Lookaside Buffer (TLB)
Aim

To implement a program that simulates a Translation Lookaside Buffer (TLB) and calculates effective memory access time with and without TLB.

Concept

A TLB is a small, fast cache that stores recently used page-to-frame mappings.

Without TLB:

EAT=Memory Access Time+Page Table Access Time

Assuming both accesses take the same time:

EAT
without
	​

=2×Memory Access Time

With a TLB:

On a TLB hit, only TLB lookup and memory access are required.
On a TLB miss, the page table must also be accessed.

A common formula is:

EAT=Hit Ratio(TLB+Memory)+(1−Hit Ratio)(TLB+2Memory)
Algorithm
Read memory access time.
Read TLB lookup time.
Read TLB hit ratio.
Calculate EAT without TLB.
Calculate TLB hit time.
Calculate TLB miss time.
Calculate overall EAT.
Display the results.
C Program
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    float memoryTime, tlbTime;
    float hitRatio;
    float eatWithoutTLB;
    float eatWithTLB;
    float hitTime, missTime;

    printf("Enter Memory Access Time (in ns): ");
    scanf("%f", &memoryTime);

    printf("Enter TLB Lookup Time (in ns): ");
    scanf("%f", &tlbTime);

    printf("Enter TLB Hit Ratio (0 to 1): ");
    scanf("%f", &hitRatio);

    if (hitRatio < 0 || hitRatio > 1) {
        printf("Invalid hit ratio!\n");
        return 0;
    }

    /*
       Without TLB:
       Page table access + actual memory access
    */
    eatWithoutTLB = 2 * memoryTime;

    /*
       TLB Hit:
       TLB lookup + memory access
    */
    hitTime = tlbTime + memoryTime;

    /*
       TLB Miss:
       TLB lookup + page table access + memory access
    */
    missTime = tlbTime + 2 * memoryTime;

    eatWithTLB =
        (hitRatio * hitTime) +
        ((1 - hitRatio) * missTime);

    printf("\n--- TLB Performance ---\n");
    printf("Memory Access Time : %.2f ns\n", memoryTime);
    printf("TLB Lookup Time    : %.2f ns\n", tlbTime);
    printf("TLB Hit Ratio      : %.2f\n", hitRatio);

    printf("\nEAT without TLB : %.2f ns\n", eatWithoutTLB);
    printf("EAT with TLB    : %.2f ns\n", eatWithTLB);

    if (eatWithTLB < eatWithoutTLB)
        printf("\nTLB improves memory access performance.\n");
    else
        printf("\nNo improvement with the given values.\n");

    return 0;
}
Sample Output
Enter Memory Access Time (in ns): 100
Enter TLB Lookup Time (in ns): 10
Enter TLB Hit Ratio (0 to 1): 0.90

--- TLB Performance ---
Memory Access Time : 100.00 ns
TLB Lookup Time    : 10.00 ns
TLB Hit Ratio      : 0.90

EAT without TLB : 200.00 ns
EAT with TLB    : 120.00 ns

TLB improves memory access performance.
Calculation

TLB hit:

10+100=110ns

TLB miss:

10+100+100=210ns

Therefore:

EAT=(0.9)(110)+(0.1)(210)
EAT=99+21=120ns

Without TLB:

EAT=100+100=200ns

Thus, the TLB reduces the effective memory access time from 200 ns to 120 ns.

Result

The TLB simulation was successfully implemented, demonstrating that a high TLB hit ratio reduces effective memory access time.

5. Memory Protection Using Base and Limit Registers
Aim

To implement a program that demonstrates memory protection using base and limit registers and prevents unauthorized memory access.

Concept

Memory protection ensures that a process cannot access memory outside its assigned region.

The valid logical-address range is:

0≤Logical Address<Limit

If the logical address is valid:

Physical Address=Base+Logical Address

Otherwise, access is denied.

Algorithm
Read the base address.
Read the limit.
Read the logical address requested by the process.
Check whether the logical address is within the limit.
If valid, calculate the physical address.
Grant access.
If invalid, deny access and display a protection error.
Stop.
C Program
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int base, limit, logicalAddress;
    int physicalAddress;

    printf("Enter Base Register: ");
    scanf("%d", &base);

    printf("Enter Limit Register: ");
    scanf("%d", &limit);

    printf("Enter Logical Address requested by process: ");
    scanf("%d", &logicalAddress);

    printf("\n--- Memory Protection Check ---\n");

    if (logicalAddress >= 0 && logicalAddress < limit) {

        physicalAddress = base + logicalAddress;

        printf("Access Status     : ALLOWED\n");
        printf("Logical Address   : %d\n", logicalAddress);
        printf("Physical Address  : %d\n", physicalAddress);
        printf("Message            : Address is within the valid range.\n");

    } else {

        printf("Access Status     : DENIED\n");
        printf("Logical Address   : %d\n", logicalAddress);
        printf("Message            : Unauthorized memory access!\n");
        printf("Reason             : Address is outside the limit.\n");
    }

    return 0;
}
